general_review.summary:
The v1 design treats articulation as "accuracy that survives more speed". It trains that with scripted read-aloud, TTS shadowing and a 0.75×/1×/1.25× speed ladder. The evidence points elsewhere.

1. Listeners judge fluency mostly by pausing and speed. What matters most is how often a speaker pauses inside a clause, rather than between clauses. Self-repairs matter little, and fluency is a construct separate from accent (Bosker et al. 2013; Pinget et al. 2014).
2. The most valid automatic markers are articulation rate and the rate of mid-clause pauses. Pause length and filled pauses mostly reflect the speaker's personal and L1 speaking style (Suzuki & Kormos 2023; Kahng 2020; de Jong et al. 2015).
3. Fluency in spontaneous speech depends mainly on how quickly the speaker builds sentences and retrieves words and chunks, not on how fast the articulators move. Separately, native-speaker studies show clear speech keeps its intelligibility advantage without slowing down (Krause & Braida 2002). A speed ladder therefore targets the wrong variable.
4. The best-supported fluency builders are:
   - Repeating the same message, which gives fluency gains of about g≈0.5 across meta-analyses. Gains transfer to new topics when the same content is repeated and generic phrases are reused (de Jong & Perfetti 2011).
   - Spacing those repetitions rather than cramming them. Massed practice gives immediate gains that vanish by one week and brings verbatim parroting (Suzuki & Hanzawa 2022). Time pressure alone erodes accuracy (Boers 2014; Tran & Saito 2021).
   - Shadowing, which helps fluency (8/8 studies), comprehensibility (7/9) and usually prosody. It does not help accent or individual segments, durability is essentially untested (1 delayed post-test in 44 studies; Whitworth & Rose 2025), and it helps production only once the learner is proficient enough (Fu et al. 2024, L2 French).
5. Transfer is skill-specific (DeKeyser 1997), and pronunciation-instruction gains shrink on spontaneous speech (Saito & Plonsky 2019). So what transfers to real conversation is practice in unscripted speaking and interaction (Sato & Lyster 2012; Freed et al. 2004 for French), measured on unseen prompts. Turn-taking latency also shapes perceived fluency (Van Os et al. 2020).

Recommended redesign: replace the speed ladder with an imitation-mode ladder:
   - delayed imitation with pitch and timing overlays, then text-visible shadowing, then blind shadowing, then retelling without text;
   - a spaced "say it again" monologue loop based on 4/3/2, with brief feedback between rounds;
   - a chunk bank for each language;
   - an interactive Q&A mode.
Build a local fluency engine from Azure word timings for both locales, computing articulation rate, mid-clause vs. boundary pauses and mean length of run relative to an L1 baseline. Azure's FluencyScore is opaque, and its ProsodyScore exists only for en-US. Report progress only on delayed, unscripted transfer prompts.

Evidence caveats:
- Most studies involve Japanese or Chinese university EFL learners or learners of L2 Dutch. Direct evidence for adult L2 French is thin (Fu et al. 2024; Freed et al. 2004; the Bibauw et al. conference data come from teenagers). The rate-vs-clarity evidence comes from native speakers.
- Method limit: the web-search budget was used up and publisher and index sites were blocked from this environment. Sources were checked through:
  - author-hosted abstracts (Bosker's site, Bibauw's site);
  - full text via GROBID TEI (Suzuki & Kormos 2023);
  - Scopus abstract mirrors (Tran & Saito 2021; Hanzawa 2021);
  - ACL Anthology XML (JEP papers);
  - a Crossref-verified study database (LinguaChat);
  - Microsoft's documentation.
- Some figures come only from independent secondary verification documents and are flagged as such: Whitworth & Rose 2025, Hamada 2016, the task-repetition meta-analyses, Saito & Plonsky 2019, and Yan et al. 2016.

findings.design_implication:
Compute fluency yourself from Azure word timings instead of using the FluencyScore:
- articulation rate (syllables per second of phonation);
- speech rate;
- mean length of run;
- silent pauses of 250 ms or more, labelled mid-clause or clause-boundary using a clause segmenter for EN and FR;
- filled pauses.

Make mid-clause pauses per 100 syllables the headline breakdown metric. Because the learner's L1 is unknown, and may be one of the two target languages, record a 60 s L1 speech baseline at onboarding. Report pause length and speed relative to that baseline.

At onboarding, and then monthly, run a 5-minute sentence-repetition probe in both EN and FR. Sentences grow from about 6 to 18 syllables and are scored by ASR word match. Add a 60 s free-speech sample.

Use the results to decide:
- which language is the L2 (or whether both are);
- the starting imitation mode (fluency-2);
- the monologue length (roughly 1 min at A levels, 2–3 min at B levels).
